<%- include('_pagehead', {
  eyebrow: 'Getting paid',
  heading: 'Card payments, into your account',
  lede: 'ConnectedWorkOS collects card payments through a payment processor you connect yourself. Today that processor is Stripe. Your money goes to your bank, we never hold it, and card numbers never touch our servers.'
}) %>

<!-- ══ Setup ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="band band--paper">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band__head">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--dark">Setting it up</p>
      <h2>Connecting Stripe, step by step</h2>
      <p>
        This is a one-time job and takes about ten minutes, most of it Stripe's own
        onboarding rather than ours. You'll want your business details and a bank account
        to hand.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="steps__item">
        <span class="steps__no">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Open Settings → Payments</h3>
          <p>Inside ConnectedWorkOS. There's one button: <em>Connect with Stripe</em>.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="steps__item">
        <span class="steps__no">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Sign in to Stripe, or create an account</h3>
          <p>If you already take card payments through Stripe, sign in and pick that account — you don't need a second one. If you don't have one, you create it here, and it's free to open.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="steps__item">
        <span class="steps__no">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Give Stripe your business details</h3>
          <p>Stripe asks the questions a payment processor is legally required to ask: your business name and structure, an address, your EIN or SSN for a sole proprietor, and the bank account for payouts. This is between you and Stripe — we never see it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="steps__item">
        <span class="steps__no">04</span>
        <div>
          <h3>You land back in ConnectedWorkOS, connected</h3>
          <p>That's it. Quotes can now take a deposit on acceptance, and invoices can be paid by card.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout callout--warn">
      <strong>Until you connect an account, you cannot take card payments.</strong> Quoting,
      scheduling and invoicing all still work, but the pay page will tell your customer that
      payment isn't available. The setup checklist on your dashboard flags this for exactly
      that reason.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ Money flow ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="field">
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Where the money goes</p>
        <h2>It is your account, not ours</h2>
        <p>
          The connection is a Stripe Connect authorisation: you own the account, you agree
          to Stripe's terms directly with Stripe, and we're permitted to create charges on
          your behalf. We never take custody of your money, and we never hold your keys.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flow">
        <div class="flow__head">Customer pays a deposit</div>
        <div class="flow__row"><span class="flow__label">Card charged</span><span class="flow__val flow__val--pay">$1,297.38</span></div>
        <div class="flow__arrow"><span>&#8595;</span> settles into</div>
        <div class="flow__head">Your Stripe account</div>
        <div class="flow__row"><span class="flow__label">Less Stripe's processing fee</span><span class="flow__val">charged by Stripe</span></div>
        <div class="flow__arrow"><span>&#8595;</span> pays out to</div>
        <div class="flow__head">Your bank account</div>
        <div class="flow__row"><span class="flow__label">On Stripe's payout schedule</span><span class="flow__val">typically 2 business days</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

<!-- ══ Fees ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="band band--tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band__head">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--dark">What it costs</p>
      <h2>Two separate bills, and we only send one</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__tag">From us</span>
        <h3>Your subscription</h3>
        <p>A flat monthly or annual price for ConnectedWorkOS, billed as part of your Connected Home Ledger plan. It does not change with how much you invoice — we take no percentage of your jobs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__tag">From Stripe</span>
        <h3>Card processing fees</h3>
        <p>Stripe charges a per-transaction fee on each card payment and deducts it before payout. Those rates are Stripe's, they're published on Stripe's pricing page, and they can change — so we don't quote a number here that might be wrong by the time you read it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="callout">
      <strong>Nobody takes a cut of your revenue except the card networks.</strong> Some
      field-service tools charge you a percentage of every invoice on top of processing.
      We don't — you pay a subscription, Stripe charges for moving money, and that's the
      whole list.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ Day to day ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="band band--paper">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band__head">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--dark">Day to day</p>
      <h2>Once it's connected</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cards cards--three">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__tag">Deposits</span>
        <h3>Collected on acceptance</h3>
        <p>Set a deposit percentage per quote. Accepting it raises the invoice and emails the pay link in the same action.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__tag">Balances</span>
        <h3>Billed when the job's done</h3>
        <p>Marking the work complete raises the remaining balance, net of the deposit. Send it, or let it sit until you're ready.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__tag">Refunds</span>
        <h3>Full or partial</h3>
        <p>Refund from the payment it belongs to. The customer gets a receipt, and your books are adjusted so the income isn't overstated.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__tag">Reconciliation</span>
        <h3>You can see the money</h3>
        <p>Every payment, with card brand and last four, a link to the Stripe receipt, and a filterable sales journal you can export as CSV for your accountant.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__tag">Security</span>
        <h3>Card data never reaches us</h3>
        <p>The payment form is Stripe's, embedded in the page. Card numbers go from your customer to Stripe directly — our servers never see or store them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__tag">Your customer</span>
        <h3>No account required</h3>
        <p>They click the link in your email, see the invoice, and pay. There's nothing to sign up for and nothing to install.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ══ Other processors ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="band band--tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band__head">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--dark">Other processors</p>
      <h2>Stripe is what's supported today</h2>
      <p>
        We're deliberately not listing processors we don't support yet. If the one you use
        matters to your decision, <a href="mailto:chris@connectedhomeoutfitters.com?subject=Payment%20processor">tell us which one</a> — that's
        the most useful signal for what we build next.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<%- include('_cta') %>
